## Why This Is Asked
Assesses augmenting a Trie to answer prefix-based queries with dynamic weights while maintaining efficient updates. Encourages careful handling of edge cases and update semantics.

## Key Concepts
- Trie augmentation with maxSubtreeWeight; non-decreasing weight updates.
- O(len(word)) insert and O(len(s)) query.
- Edge cases: empty word input, overlapping prefixes, no matches.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.wordWeight = 0;
    this.hasWord = false;
    this.maxSubtreeWeight = 0;
  }
}
class PrefixWeightTrie {
  constructor() { this.root = new Node(); }
  insert(word, weight) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new Node());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    if (!node.hasWord || weight > node.wordWeight) {
      node.hasWord = true;
      node.wordWeight = weight;
    }
    // update maxSubtreeWeight along path (omitted detailed propagation for brevity)
  }
  queryLongestPrefix(s) {
    let node = this.root;
    let bestLen = 0, bestWeight = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
      const c = s[i];
      if (!node.next.has(c)) break;
      node = node.next.get(c);
      if (node.maxSubtreeWeight > 0) {
        bestLen = i + 1;
        bestWeight = node.maxSubtreeWeight;
      }
    }
    return [bestLen, bestWeight];
  }
}
```

## Why This Question Matters

This problem evaluates a candidate's ability to extend standard grid DP problems with additional state dimensions. Modeling exact-turn constraints is essential in path-planning scenarios such as robot navigation, vehicle routing, and game movement where turn penalties or restrictions exist.

## Core Concepts

- **State Design**: Extending traditional grid DP with turn count and last direction dimensions
- **Transition Logic**: Increment turn count when direction changes between moves
- **Space Optimization**: Using row-wise or 2-layer rolling arrays to reduce memory from O(n*m*K) to O(m*K)
- **Edge Cases**: Handling obstacles, impossible turn requirements, and boundary conditions

## Reference Implementation

```javascript
function maxPathSumWithTurns(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const INF = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: m }, () =>
      Array.from({ length: K + 1 }, () => [INF, INF])
    )
  );

  dp[0][0][0][0] = dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      if (grid[i][j] === -1) continue;
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        if (j + 1 < m && grid[i][j+1] !== -1) {
          const newTurn = t + (i > 0 ? 1 : 0);
          if (newTurn <= K) {
            dp[i][j+1][newTurn][0] = Math.max(
              dp[i][j+1][newTurn][0],
              dp[i][j][t][1] + grid[i][j+1]
            );
          }
          dp[i][j+1][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i][j+1][t][0], dp[i][j][t][0] + grid[i][j+1]);
        }
        if (i + 1 < n && grid[i+1][j] !== -1) {
          const newTurn = t + (j > 0 ? 1 : 0);
          if (newTurn <= K) {
            dp[i+1][j][newTurn][1] = Math.max(
              dp[i+1][j][newTurn][1],
              dp[i][j][t][0] + grid[i+1][j]
            );
          }
          dp[i+1][j][t][1] = Math.max(dp[i+1][j][t][1], dp[i][j][t][1] + grid[i+1][j]);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const result = Math.max(...dp[n-1][m-1].flat());
  return result === INF ? -1 : result;
}
```

